# Chapter 4, seventh part: Global, Options and Global, Named.
# Part of the chapter source.

.h2 Options [format-options]
.ix Global Options
.ix Options panel
.ix Display options
.ix Expanded Status Line
.ix Display On-Screen Help
.ix Display Tab and End-of-Line Characters
.ix Display Dots for Spaces
.ix Backups Kept
.ix BAK file
.ix Backup file
.ix config file
Global, Options holds the settings of the Editor itself: how much of the
screen the Editor draws and whether it keeps a backup when it saves.  They
belong to the user and not to the document: nothing of this panel is saved
with a document, and a setting stays as it is for every document, now and in
later sessions.

.h3 How to reach the command [format-options-reach]
Press {k:F12}, choose Global and then Options (the fifth entry; the hint line
reads "Other settings"), as described in {x:format-global-menu}.  The panel
has no neighbors: {k:Ins} accepts and {k:Esc} cancels.
.pic pictures/format-options.png 5 The Options panel.

.h3 The fields [format-options-fields]
.ix Options, fields
The panel has eleven rows: four choices ({k:Left}, {k:Right} and {k:Space}
change them), one number and six information rows.  An information row shows a
fixed value; the cursor skips it and it cannot be changed.
.table 28 7 22
Field | Default | What it does
Expanded Status Line? | No | A second row of the status.
Display On-Screen Help? | Yes | The two rows of key labels.
Display Tab & End-of-Line Characters? | Yes | Marks in the text.
Display Dots for Spaces? | No | A dot for each space.
Backups Kept (0 to 99) | 3 | Numbered backups on Save.
.end
The six information rows are these.  They are not errors and they do not show
a count or a size of the document: each one is a setting that has one value
here, which is what the row says, and the value is not changed by anything.
.table 22 12 26
Row | Shows | What it means here
Block Indicators | Solid Lines | The screen draws a solid line between two blocks.
Display Even Justification? | No | The screen never spreads a line of text to the
 full width; the Printer does it for the blocks that are justified.
Display +/- with Section Tags? | No | A section tag has no sign in front of it;
 the outline commands show which sections are open ({n:edit-b-outlines}).
Units | Inches (in) | The lengths of the panels are in inches, and the fields
 that have a Unit field say which unit they use.
Line Weight (Borders) | 100 % | Borders print at the weight that they have.
(Other) | 100 % | Lines of the Line marker print at the weight of the marker.
.end
{b:Expanded Status Line?}  With Yes the screen has a second row under the
status line that shows the format of the block at the cursor: the justification,
the indent, the left block indent (L), and the word Headline when the block is
a headline, such as
.code
 Just: Even    Indent: None           L: 0
.end
The status line and this row are described in {x:format-screen-status}.  The
screen has one row fewer for the text when the row is on.

{b:Display On-Screen Help?}  With Yes the last two rows of the screen hold the
labels of the function keys ({k:F1} Help, {k:F2} Split, and so on); in the
graphical version, holding {k:Alt} or {k:Ctrl} shows the labels of the Alt and
Ctrl keys instead.  With No the two rows are used for text, and a message of
the Editor takes one row at the foot when there is one.

{b:Display Tab & End-of-Line Characters?}  With Yes the end of every line of a
block is marked with a small left pointing triangle and every tab with a right
pointing arrow, drawn dimmed, so that line ends and tabs can be seen.  With No
neither is drawn.  The marks are not text: they are not saved, not printed, and
the cursor does not stop on them.

{b:Display Dots for Spaces?}  With Yes every space of the text is drawn as a
small centered dot, to see how many spaces there are between two words; with
No a space is blank.  It is not saved and not printed.

{b:Backups Kept (0 to 99)}  The number of numbered backups that Save keeps of
the file that it replaces, such as {c:.backup/report.bak01} (the newest) and
{c:.backup/report.bak02}.  0 makes none and the default is 3.  A save of a
document
that has not changed makes no backup.  A lower number puts a question first, and the box
shows which backups are removed ({n:file-backup-option}).  {n:file} (File, Save) has the whole
of Save.  Autosave and its interval are set in the Setup of the Document
Manager ({n:str}).

.h3 Where the settings are kept [format-options-kept]
.ix Config file, Options
.ix expanded_status, onscreen_help
.ix show_marks, show_dots, backup
.ix STRATA_HOME
When {k:Ins} is pressed the five choices take effect at once and are saved in
the file {c:config} in the Strata directory of the user, which is {c:.strata}
in the home directory, or the directory that the environment variable
{c:STRATA_HOME} names.  The file is a list of lines, key and value.  The Editor
reads it again at start.
.table 28 16 13
Field | Key | Written as
Expanded Status Line? | {c:expanded_status} | yes or no
Display On-Screen Help? | {c:onscreen_help} | yes or no
Display Tab & End-of-Line Characters? | {c:show_marks} | yes or no
Display Dots for Spaces? | {c:show_dots} | yes or no
Backups Kept (0 to 99) | backups | 0 to 99
.end
The same file holds the settings of the Document Manager ({n:str} and
{n:appcfg}).  A key that is missing has its default.  If the file cannot be
written (there is no home directory, the directory cannot be made, it belongs
to somebody else, or the disk does not accept the write), a box titled Options
shows the reason
({n:format-messages}); the settings still hold for the rest of the
session.  After {k:Esc} nothing is changed.

.note The screen is drawn again when the panel is accepted, so that a change
  of the status rows or of the marks shows at once.  The settings of Options
  are not a change of the document: the star in front of the name of the file
  does not appear.

.h2 Named [format-named]
.ix Global Named
.ix Named block format
.ix Named Block Format panel
.ix Block format, named
.ix Retrieve Named Block
.ix Save as, named format
Global, Named keeps named formats: a group of settings for a block that is
given a name and is saved with the document, so that a block can say "format
me as Quotation".  You can make, change, copy and delete the named formats
of a document.  A block that has a named format takes its attributes,
justification, indent, tab stops, left block indent, font, line spacing and
block spacing from it, at the place of the lookup order of
{x:format-order}.

.h3 How to reach the commands [format-named-reach]
Press {k:F12}, choose Global and then Named (the sixth entry; the hint line
reads "Edit, Delete named block format settings") as described in
{x:format-global-menu}.  A second menu offers two entries: Edit, "Edit a
named block format" and Delete, "Delete a named block format".  Choose with
{k:Left}, {k:Right} and {k:Return}, or press {k:E} or {k:D}.
.pic pictures/format-named.png 5 The Named Block Format panel.

.h3 Edit [format-named-edit]
.ix Edit, Named
.ix (new), named format
.ix Named Block Format, fields
If the document has named formats a list titled Retrieve Named Block appears
first.  Its first entry is (new), for a format that does not exist yet, and the
others are the names of the formats; choose one with the cursor keys and
{k:Return}, or leave with {k:Esc}.  A document with no named format goes
directly to the panel.  The panel is titled Named Block Format.  Every field
starts empty (a choice shows [], a text field is blank), because a named
format sets only what it names: an empty field is left to the other levels of
the lookup ({n:format-order}).  When a format was retrieved the fields show
its values and the name appears in Save as.
.table 22 38
Field | Values
Save as | a name
Ruler (tab stops) | text, such as 5,20R
Attributes | [], Normal, Bold, Italic, Underscore Words
Justification | [], Left, Right, Center, Even
Indent | [], None, First, All but First, All
Font | [] and the twenty font names
Line Spacing | empty, or a number of lines, such as 1.50
Block Spacing | empty, or a number of lines, such as 2.00
Block Indent (Left) | whole number
Block Indent (Right) | whole number
Vertical Centering (%) | whole number
Winding Columns | whole number from 1 to 9; {k:F2} opens the layout
Keep Block on One Page? | [], No, Yes
.end
{b:Winding Columns.}  With {k:F2} (or {k:Keypad +}) on the field the Winding
Column Layout panel opens, as on the Global Format panel
({n:format-fields-spacing}): Number of Columns, Gutter Size (an inch with up
to three decimals) and Vertical Rules (a choice that starts with [], then
None, Single, Double and Wide).  A block that has the named format is set in
that number of columns with that gutter and that rule; an empty gutter and
[] leave the gutter and the rule to the level of the block and to the
document.  The Editor shows one column; the Printer sets the columns.
.pic pictures/format-named-winding.png 5 The Winding Column Layout panel of the Named Block Format, with two columns, a gutter of 0.5 inch and a single rule.
The attributes, the justification, the indent, the tab stops and the left
block indent are used on the screen and on the printed page; the font is used
on the printed page ({n:format-set-typefaces}), and so are the line spacing
and the block spacing, in a final print ({n:format-set-spacing}).

{b:Accepting.}  {k:Ins} checks the panel: Save as must not be empty (the box
shows "Enter a name to save the format as") and must not have control
characters, Block Indent (Left) must be digits only, and Line Spacing and
Block Spacing must be numbers with at most two decimals; otherwise a box
shows "Invalid number" and the panel is shown again with what was typed.  The
Ruler (tab stops) is not checked.  {k:Esc} leaves everything as it was.

{b:Saving.}  The format is saved under the name in Save as.  If the name is that
of a format that exists the old definition is replaced; if it is a new name
the format is a copy and the one that was retrieved stays.  So a format is
renamed by saving it under another name and deleting the old one.  The
formats are kept in the order of their names (by character code, so that
capitals come before lower case letters).  The document is marked as changed.

.h3 Delete [format-named-delete]
.ix Delete, Named
.ix Delete Named Block
Delete shows the list titled Delete Named Block with the names of the formats.
Choose one with the cursor keys and {k:Return}; the question "Delete the named
block format NAME?" is asked as a list of two entries, Yes and No: Yes deletes
the format, and No or {k:Esc} keeps it.
When the document has no named formats a box shows "There are no named block
formats" and nothing else happens.  Blocks that used the name are not changed:
they take the next level of the lookup.

.h3 Limits [format-named-limits]
.ix Named format, limits
.table 24 36
Case | What happens
More than 40 named formats | Only the first 40 are listed.
A name longer than 31 characters | It is cut in the lists, so that the format
 cannot be retrieved by its list entry.  Keep names short.
A name that has the same letters in another case | It is another name.
.end
